I'll use Python to parse the C# code and extract the comments:

```python
import re

def extract_asserts(code):
    matches = re.findall(r"// @@@ (.+)", code)
    return ["Debug.Assert(" + match + ")"; match in matches]

cs_code = """
﻿using System;
// @@@ Value of x increases by y in the function
public static int Sum(int x, int y)
{
    for (int i = 0; i < y; i++){
        x.Inc(1);
    }
    return x;
}
"""

asserts = extract_asserts(cs_code)
for assert_stmt in asserts:
    print("<code>" + assert_stmt + "</code>")
```
This Python program will find all occurrences of "// @@@" in the specified C# code and print it as an Assert statement in a <code> block, which could look something like <code>Debug.Assert(OLD(x) + y = x)</code>. The actual logic inside the assert statement is the text after "// @@@".
